<section id="recorded-votes"><h2>What the recorded votes show</h2>
<p>These are two documented roll calls, not a complete voting record. Other candidates were not voting members of this Council. Absence from the table is not a vote against or support for either motion.</p>
<div class="election-table-scroll"><table><caption>Current Council members who are on the 2026 ballot</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Candidate</th><th scope="col">March 25: rescind support for solar proposal submission</th><th scope="col">October 14: Council representation clarification</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">Mike Mercieca</th><td>For rescinding</td><td>Opposed</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Kevin Burke</th><td>Opposed rescinding</td><td>For</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Thoma Crabs</th><td>Opposed rescinding</td><td>For</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Merri-Ann Hobbs</th><td>Opposed rescinding</td><td>For</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><strong>Solar, motion 2026-083:</strong> the proposal to rescind resolution 2025-287 was defeated 1–6. That earlier resolution supported submission of a long-term energy project proposal. This was not a vote granting final project approval. Hobbs moved the rescission motion but the roll call records her voting against it; a mover or seconder is not a substitute for the recorded vote. <a href="https://www.sables-spanish.ca/wp-content/uploads/2026/03/Council-Minutes-March-25-2026-with-attachments.pdf#page=1">March 25 minutes, motion 2026-083</a>.</p>
<p><strong>Representation, motion 2026-297:</strong> Council voted 6–1 to state that Mercieca’s attendance at Queen’s Park did not represent Council and that no Township funds were expensed for the attendance. This is what Council’s posted minutes state; it is not an independent expense audit. The candidate’s explanation has not been added to this guide. <a href="https://www.sables-spanish.ca/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/Council-Minutes-October-14-2026-with-attachments.pdf#page=1">October 14 posted minutes, motions 2026-295 and 2026-297</a>.</p>
</section>
<section id="issues"><h2>The municipal issues to compare</h2>
<h3>Taxes and the financial baseline</h3><p>The final 2026 bylaw sets the municipal general-purpose levy at <strong>$5,492,564</strong>. The March committee draft used $5,388,450. Those are different stages of the budget; the draft’s proposed percentage increase should not be presented as the final increase. <a href="https://www.sables-spanish.ca/wp-content/uploads/2026/06/2026-26-Taxation-Rates.pdf#page=2">2026 tax bylaw, pages 1–2</a>; <a href="https://www.sables-spanish.ca/wp-content/uploads/2026/03/Council-Minutes-March-25-2026-with-attachments.pdf">March Finance Committee draft in the Council packet</a>.</p>
<p>The 2025 auditor gave an unmodified opinion. The statements report <strong>$706,199 annual surplus</strong>, <strong>$1,319,885 municipal debt</strong> and <strong>$5,302,026 net financial assets</strong>. Accumulated surplus of $27,980,626 includes the municipality’s capital position and is not a cash balance available to spend. An audit opinion is not a verdict on every policy decision. <a href="https://www.sables-spanish.ca/wp-content/uploads/2026/06/2025-Audited-Financial-Statements.pdf#page=7">2025 audited financial statements</a>.</p>
<p>October’s allocation of a $1,237,176 operating surplus is a different reported measure from the audited annual surplus. The minutes allocate $371,153 to tax-rate stabilization and $866,023 in total to working capital. A reconciliation of the two surplus measures remains an open question. <a href="https://www.sables-spanish.ca/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/Council-Minutes-October-14-2026-with-attachments.pdf#page=1">October 14 posted minutes, motions 2026-295 and 2026-297</a>.</p>
<p><strong>Ask each candidate:</strong> Which services would you protect or change, what would the annual cost be, and how would you pay for it?</p>
<h3>Roads, bridges, water and long-term maintenance</h3><p>The 2025 asset plan estimates infrastructure replacement value at <strong>$303.6 million</strong>, with 80% rated fair or better. Some ratings rely on age rather than inspection. It estimates a roughly <strong>$440,000 annual tax-funded capital shortfall</strong>, using rounded annual need of $1.7 million and sustainable funding of $1.27 million. These are planning estimates, not a current debt bill. The suggested 1% annual tax-revenue increase dedicated to capital over a 20-year phase-in is a recommendation, not an adopted election promise. <a href="https://www.sables-spanish.ca/wp-content/uploads/2025/08/Sables-Spanish-Rivers-AMP-2025-Final.pdf#page=5">2025 asset management plan, PDF pages 5–6</a>.</p>
<p><strong>Ask each candidate:</strong> Which projects come first, what evidence sets the order, and what mix of taxes, grants, reserves and borrowing would you use? How will water and wastewater users see the effect on their bills?</p>
<h3>Solar development and local decision-making</h3><p>The March recorded vote above is one verified point in the proposal’s history. A candidate’s current position, conditions for support, and position on municipal agreements still need a dated answer. The Circle also maintains a separate <a href="/land/massey-solar-project">Massey solar project record</a>.</p>
<p><strong>Ask each candidate:</strong> What is your present position, what conditions matter, and which decisions are actually within Township authority? Provide the documents behind your answer.</p>
<h3>Open meetings and accountability</h3><p>The Ontario Ombudsman found that the reviewed February 2024 and February 2025 closed discussions fit the personal-matters exception, and the January 2025 pre-meeting conversation did not meet the statutory definition of a meeting. It nevertheless found a procedural contravention: the February 2025 closing resolution omitted a third discussion item. The recommendations call for compliance and complete descriptions of closed-session items. <a href="https://ombudsman.on.ca/sites/default/files/documents/Ombudsman%20Ontario-Sables-Spanish%20Rivers-March%202026-Report-accessible.pdf#page=9">Ombudsman report, March 2026, paragraphs 41–46</a>.</p>
<p><strong>Ask each candidate:</strong> How will you make agendas, decisions, conflicts, spending and follow-up easier for residents to track? How will you implement the Ombudsman’s recommendations?</p>
<h3>Clinic services, housing and community priorities</h3><p>The adopted strategic plan covers quality of life, community appearance, infrastructure, development and tourism, and municipal governance. Its June 2024–June 2025 progress report records physician recruitment, expanded foot-care services, housing discussions with the district services board, and emergency-service cooperation including Sagamok. These are the Township’s reported actions for that period, not a current service-capacity audit. <a href="https://www.sables-spanish.ca/wp-content/uploads/2024/06/Final-2024-2028-Strategic-Plan.pdf">2024–2028 strategic plan</a>; <a href="https://www.sables-spanish.ca/wp-content/uploads/2025/12/Strategic-Plan-Annual-Report-2024-to-2025.pdf#page=3">June 2024–June 2025 progress report</a>.</p>
<p><strong>Ask each candidate:</strong> What can the municipality deliver during the next term, with whom, at what cost, and how will residents measure progress? Include all parts of the township in your answer.</p>
</section>
<section id="submit"><h2>Candidates: submit your platform</h2>
<p>Candidates for mayor and councillor are invited to supply their current platform. Everyone receives the same questions and space. A campaign website, a public document or a concise response of up to 500 words is welcome. Include your name, the office you are seeking, the date, and a public campaign link we can use to verify the submission.</p>
<ol><li>What are your three priorities, and what would residents see completed in the first year?</li><li>What experience prepares you for this office? Please provide public links that verify it.</li><li>How would you pay for your priorities? State the likely tax, fee, reserve, grant or borrowing implications.</li><li>How would you prioritize roads, bridges, water and wastewater work?</li><li>What is your position on the Massey solar proposal, including any conditions and limits on municipal authority?</li><li>What would you change about transparency, public participation and Council decision-making?</li><li>What would you do on clinic access, housing, local economic development and cooperation with neighbouring communities?</li></ol><p>Use the <a href="/contact">Circle contact form</a> and begin your message with <strong>“Sables-Spanish Rivers election 2026”</strong>. You can send a platform link if the form is too short. Please state whether you authorize publication of your answers under your name. Contact details stay private. Submissions are reviewed for identity, relevance and sourcing before publication; receipt does not mean immediate publication.</p>
<p>Candidate statements will be identified as their statements, dated, and kept separate from independently checked records. Corrections and updated answers are welcome. A missing answer will remain labelled as missing; it will not be interpreted as a policy position.</p>
</section>
<section id="voting"><h2>Voting and assistance</h2>
<p>Online voting closes <strong>Monday, October 26 at 8 pm Eastern</strong>. Use your official voter information letter. For a missing letter, eligibility or ballot problem, contact the Township Clerk at <a href="tel:+17058652646">705-865-2646</a>. The election page and Clerk control the official instructions. <a href="https://www.sables-spanish.ca/2026-municipal-elections/">Township election page</a>; <a href="https://www.sables-spanish.ca/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/VOTE-ONLINE-Information-Flyer-2026.pdf">Township voting information flyer</a>.</p>
<div class="election-table-scroll"><table><caption>Library assistance, updated October 14</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Location</th><th scope="col">Published weekday hours</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">Massey Library, 185 Grove Street</th><td>Monday, Tuesday, Thursday and Friday: 10 am–5 pm. Wednesday: 11 am–7 pm.</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Webbwood Library, 16 Main Street</th><td>Monday and Tuesday: 11 am–4 pm. Wednesday: 11 am–4 pm and 6–8 pm. Thursday: 5–8 pm. Friday: 10 am–2 pm.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The poster covers advance dates October 14–16 and 19–23, and election day October 26. Both libraries are closed on weekends. <a href="https://www.sables-spanish.ca/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/New-Library-Voting-Hours-October-14-2026-1024x791.png">updated October 14 library hours</a>.</p>
<p>The September voting flyer gives Township-office assistance as 10 am–3 pm during the advance period and 9 am–3 pm plus 6–8 pm on election day, with a noon–1 pm closure. Verify any last-minute changes with the Clerk. The office is at 11 Birch Lake Road, Massey. <a href="https://www.sables-spanish.ca/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/VOTE-ONLINE-Information-Flyer-2026.pdf">Township voting information flyer</a>.</p>
<p>The school-board declarations list the relevant trustees as acclaimed: Robert Clement (Rainbow, Area 8), Lindsay Liske and Kathleen Rosilius (Huron-Superior Catholic regional seats), Rachelle Beaubien (Grand Nord), and Paul Gervais (Nouvelon). School-board entitlement depends on the elector’s designation. Follow the official ballot and ask the Clerk about any discrepancy. <a href="https://www.sables-spanish.ca/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Declaration-of-Acclamation-to-Office-English-Public-School-Board.pdf">English public declaration</a>; <a href="https://www.sables-spanish.ca/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/School-Board-Acclamations-2026.pdf">other board declarations</a>.</p>
</section>
<section id="method"><h2>How this guide is compiled</h2>
<p>This guide uses the Clerk’s certified list, official Council records, municipal financial and planning documents, and the Ombudsman’s report. Candidate statements, when received, will form a separate evidence class. Candidates appear in the certified list’s surname order within each office. No endorsement, score or ranking is assigned.</p>
<p>The archive includes available 2025 and 2026 meeting packets through October 14. The analysis above examines selected relevant decisions; it is not an exhaustive attendance, voting or full-term performance assessment. No occupation, criminal record, family connection or affiliation has been inferred from a shared name. Current platform searches have not established a complete authenticated set.</p>
<p>Known source discrepancies are preserved: June voting procedures say online voting began at 9 am, while September’s flyer says 10 am; both agree on the October 26 closing time. The later October 14 poster supplies updated library hours. The August local candidate report calls St. Denis an MPP; the primary parliamentary record identifies his service as an MP. Draft and adopted budget figures are kept separate.</p>
<p>Send corrections through <a href="/contact">the contact form</a>, with the claim, source link and date. Changes to a published profile should retain a dated correction note. This is an independent guide, not an official Township election service.</p>
</section>

